comments.body:
Hey @uhyo and React maintainers,

I’ve reproduced the behavior locally with a minimal React 19.3.0 page (see the attached test index.html). The result indeed flips between 4 (DOCUMENT_POSITION_FOLLOWING) and 32 (DOCUMENT_POSITION_IMPLEMENTATION_SPECIFIC) on every other render, confirming that this is a real regression.

What’s happening?
The regression originates from the fiber‑tree validation added in #34069 (validateDocumentPositionWithFiberTree). The helpers that check the preceding and following relationships (isFiberPrecedingCheck / isFiberFollowingCheck in packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberTreeReflection.js) compare fibers by strict identity:

```
if (child === boundary) { … }
if (child === target) { … }
```

When a commit occurs, the fiber cached on the DOM node (target) can belong to the alternate tree, while the _fragmentFiber stored on the FragmentInstance (boundary) belongs to the current tree. Because the identity check doesn’t consider alternate, the traversal fails to find a match on odd renders and falls back to DOCUMENT_POSITION_IMPLEMENTATION_SPECIFIC (value 32)—the alternate pair swaps on each commit, which explains the every‑other‑render toggle.

Suggested fix
Update the preceding/following checks to handle the alternate fiber, mirroring the CONTAINED_BY branch that already does so:

js
```
function isFiberPrecedingCheck(child, boundary) {
  return (
    child === boundary ||
    (child.alternate && child.alternate === boundary)
  );
}
function isFiberFollowingCheck(child, target) {
  return (
    child === target ||
    (child.alternate && child.alternate === target)
  );
}
```
Or, more generically, introduce a helper:

js
```
function fibersMatch(a, b) {
  return a === b || (a && a.alternate === b) || (b && b.alternate === a);
}
```
Replace strict comparisons throughout the validation logic.

